# Git Conventions

## Commit Authorship

### No AI co-authors

Never add an AI coding agent as a co-author. Commits must credit humans only.

```text
❌ Incorrect:
Co-authored-by: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Co-authored-by: Cursor Agent <cursoragent@cursor.com>
Co-authored-by: Cursor <cursoragent@cursor.com>

✅ Correct:
Co-authored-by: Malek Gara-Hellal <hi@findmalek.com>
```

Rules for agents:

- Do not append a `Co-authored-by:` trailer for yourself when committing.
- If a trailer was added automatically (some agent runtimes inject one), strip it before the commit is pushed.
- Do not add trailers for review bots (`gemini-code-assist`, `coderabbitai`) either — they reviewed the change, they did not write it.
- Automation identities that genuinely author commits (`dependabot[bot]`, `Languine Bot`) stay.

Why it matters: squash-merging re-collects every `Co-authored-by:` trailer in a PR, so a single leftover line puts an AI agent in the repository's contributor list permanently.

### Enforcement

- `.github/workflows/pr-no-ai-coauthors.yml` fails any PR whose commits carry a denied trailer.
- Check locally before pushing: `./scripts/check-ai-coauthors.sh`
- Fix by rewriting the offending messages, not by disabling the check:

```bash
git commit --amend                                 # last commit only
git rebase -i $(git merge-base HEAD origin/main)   # several commits
```

## Branches

- Name branches with the issue number: `type/NNN-slug` (e.g. `fix/250-stock-race-condition`) or `NNN-slug`.
- `.github/workflows/pr-link-issue.yml` reads that number and appends `Closes #N` to the PR body when the PR targets `main` and has no closing reference yet.

## Pull Requests

- Link the issue in the PR description (`Closes #N`) instead of relying on the auto-link fallback.
- Keep changes scoped to the issue; do not bundle unrelated working-tree edits into the commit.
- Follow `.github/pull_request_template.md` and complete its checklist.
